# Normalise line endings in the repository, so a clone is the same on any platform.
# Without this, git's autocrlf setting decides what a checkout looks like, and the
# same file differs between a Windows and a Linux working copy.
#
# It matters most for tests/fixtures/**, pinned below: several fixtures are compared
# as text, and a fixture whose endings change between checkouts makes a test pass on
# one machine and fail on another for a reason nothing in the diff shows.
#
# It matters for the declaration too. The report's declarationFingerprint hashes the
# file with its newlines normalised, precisely so a CRLF checkout and an LF one produce
# the same fingerprint - but that normalisation is a safety net, and pinning the endings
# is what keeps it from being load bearing.
#
# (The ported version of this file justified itself by citing Get-TextFingerprint and
# a Jenkinsfile read from a commit. Neither exists here, so the reason was replaced with
# one that is true of this codebase - and replaced again when the PowerShell went.)
* text=auto eol=lf

*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.xml  text eol=lf

# Fixtures are compared byte for byte by the tests, so their endings are pinned.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf
